course: dictionary
lesson: 9188
title: Socialisation: learning to live with others
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how people learn. Socialisation is one of them. Look at the context: «In its first few months a puppy should meet many people, animals, sounds and things in a calm way; this is called socialisation.» The words "this is called" point to the meaning: it is the name for that process of meeting and learning.
explain: Socialisation is not only for puppies. A child learns how to behave with other people from family, school and friends: sharing, manners, language and rules. The clue is "with other people": you learn by living alongside others, not only from lessons.
explain: The sentence about the dog gives a clue from the other side. «A dog that met few things when young may be fearful later.» If a young animal does not get enough of this learning, it may grow up scared. So when you meet a new word, ask what happens when it is missing.
fact: Socialisation is the way a person or animal learns to behave with others and fit in.
fact: For a child, socialisation covers sharing, manners, language, rules and customs.
fact: For a puppy or kitten, socialisation means getting used to people, other animals, sounds and places, so it is calm and friendly.
fact: «Early socialisation» means this learning in the first months or years of life.
fact: In a formal sense, socialisation can mean putting a business or service under the control of the state or the community.
exercise: «In its first few months a puppy should meet many people, animals, sounds and things in a calm way.» What is socialisation for a puppy? | Sleeping for most of the day | *Getting used to people, animals and sounds so it is calm and friendly | Being sold to a new owner | Learning to fetch a stick only
exercise: Mia says socialisation only happens in a classroom. Is she right? | No, it is only for dogs | *No, children learn it from family, friends and school life together | Yes, it is the same as sleeping | Yes, it is only taught in lessons
exercise: Which phrase from the lesson means not enough of this learning? | socialisation is a noun | puppy socialisation classes | *lack of socialisation | early socialisation
test: Which phrase from the lesson means not enough of this learning? | socialisation classes | puppy socialisation | early socialisation | *lack of socialisation
test: What does socialisation mean when it describes a business? | Making it smaller and cheaper | *Putting it under the control of the state or community | Painting the building in bright colours | Opening a new shop in a busy street
exam: Which best describes socialisation for a child? | Counting coins and paying for things | Learning to swim and ride a bike | *Learning how to behave with other people, including sharing, manners and rules | Keeping a diary of what you did each day
